      "id": "fskn7yn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Factors Responsible for Soil Formation"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Soil formation depends on climate, parent rock material, topography, biological factors, and time.",
        "Alluvial soil is the most fertile and widely distributed soil in India, divided into Khadar (new) and Bhangar (old).",
        "Black soil, also known as Regur, possesses high moisture-retention capacity and is ideal for growing cotton.",
        "Laterite soil forms under tropical conditions of high temperature and heavy rainfall through the process of intense leaching.",
        "Problematic soils include saline, alkaline, and acidic soils, which require chemical amendments and proper management for cultivation."
      ],
      "id": "gnbiug2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1zeifs0",
      "content": "Soil formation, also called pedogenesis, depends upon several interacting factors."
    },
