    {
      "id": "bvvbxj9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Alluvial Soil – Detailed Explanation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zhuqog7",
      "content": "Alluvial soil is the most widespread and agriculturally important soil in India. It is formed by the deposition of sediments brought by rivers such as the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra. The fertility of this soil makes the Northern Plains the food bowl of India."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Soil Type",
        "Key Formation / Characteristics",
        "Suitable Crops"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Alluvial Soil",
          "Deposited by river systems; rich in potash, poor in phosphorus",
          "Rice, Wheat, Sugarcane, Pulses"
        ],
        [
          "Black Soil (Regur)",
          "Derived from basalt/volcanic lava; high clay content and moisture retention",
          "Cotton, Soybean, Groundnut"
        ],
        [
          "Laterite Soil",
          "Formed by intense leaching due to heavy tropical rainfall; rich in iron oxides",
          "Tea, Coffee, Cashew, Rubber"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "6h6ttty"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ljcueea",
      "content": "Khadar is newer and more fertile due to annual flooding, whereas Bhangar is older and contains calcareous deposits known as kankar."
    },
    {
      "id": "31doklg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Black Soil – Why Ideal for Cotton?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a04ecye",
      "content": "Black soil has high moisture retention capacity due to its clay content. During dry seasons it develops deep cracks that allow aeration, a property known as self-ploughing. These characteristics make it highly suitable for cotton cultivation."
    },
    {
      "id": "e7t15yh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Laterite Soil – Leaching Process"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yuw8a6y",
      "content": "Laterite soils form under conditions of high temperature and heavy rainfall. Intense leaching removes silica and bases, leaving iron and aluminium oxides behind. With fertilizers and irrigation, these soils become productive."
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Differentiate between Khadar (fertile, floodplain alluvium) and Bhangar (older, less fertile alluvium with kankar nodule deposits).",
        "Understand the intense leaching process of Laterite soil, which washes away silica leaving iron and aluminium compounds.",
        "Note that Black soil develops deep cracks during dry seasons, leading to a self-ploughing phenomenon."
      ],
      "id": "t1qcnv3"
    },
